        "comments": "Bacteria and source DNA available from McCutcheon Lab.\n\nThis endosymbiotic bacterium typically consists of a single, highly polyploid (a few hundred copies of the genome) chromosome. In this case however, the single chromosome has split into several dozen lineages, each with a different reduced version of the ancestral single chromosome. The chromosomes were named variantX to distinguish them from each other. Some of the chromosomes are complete circular molecules, while others are likely circular molecules but they still have a run of N's at the end of the component contig to close the gap.\nThe annotation was added by the NCBI Prokaryotic Genome Annotation Pipeline (PGAP). Information about PGAP can be found here: https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/genome/annotation_prok/",
